// Operator resolution as rules (my slice off ADR 0028's engine, 2026-09-19).
//
// ONE knowledge, one place. Today the same table is written three times: sema's
// `op_trait` map (16 binary + 2 unary), mono_clone's own copy (11 + 2, no
// bitwise), and mlir_gen's comparison fast paths with their own element lists.
// The copies HAVE diverged, measured; these rules derive the divergence instead
// of leaving it to be noticed.
//
// Types are flat canonical strings, pre-substituted by the caller — the same
// convention traits.dl states, so the two rule sets can share a fact universe.
//
// Facts come from the tree, not from this file: op_trait from sema_expr.cpp's
// table, trait_method from the stdlib trait DECLARATIONS, impl_method from the
// collected impls, mono_desugars from mono_clone.cpp's copy.

// ── inputs ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// The operator token and the trait sema routes it to.
.decl op_trait(op: symbol, t: symbol)
// A method the trait DECLARES. A trait with no methods has no row here:
// `pub trait PartialOrd {}` is exactly that case.
.decl trait_method(t: symbol, m: symbol)
// The method sema derives from the operator token (`<` -> lt), carried as a
// fact because it is a naming convention, not a property of the trait.
.decl op_method(op: symbol, m: symbol)
// A type that implements a method.
.decl impl_method(ty: symbol, m: symbol)
// mono_clone's SECOND copy of the operator table.
.decl mono_desugars(op: symbol, m: symbol)
.input op_trait, trait_method, op_method, impl_method, mono_desugars

// ── what the trait actually offers ──────────────────────────────────────
// The operator's own method is declared by the trait it is routed to.
.decl covered(op: symbol, t: symbol)
.output covered
covered(Op, T) :- op_trait(Op, T), op_method(Op, M), trait_method(T, M).

// Sema routes the operator to a trait that declares no such method. Measured:
// the four order operators — `Ord` declares `cmp` alone, and no type in stdlib
// implements lt/le/gt/ge.
.decl uncovered(op: symbol, t: symbol)
.output uncovered
uncovered(Op, T) :- op_trait(Op, T), !covered(Op, T).

// ── the second copy, and where it disagrees ─────────────────────────────
// Sema desugars the operator and mono does not. Measured: the bitwise five.
.decl mono_gap(op: symbol, t: symbol)
.output mono_gap
mono_gap(Op, T) :- op_trait(Op, T), !mono_has(Op).
.decl mono_has(op: symbol)
mono_has(Op) :- mono_desugars(Op, _).

// ── reachability for a concrete type ────────────────────────────────────
// The type implements some other method of the same trait, so asking about
// this one is meaningful rather than a cartesian product.
.decl trait_touched(ty: symbol, t: symbol)
trait_touched(Ty, T) :- trait_method(T, Om), impl_method(Ty, Om).

// The operator is covered by its trait, the type has that trait in play, and
// the specific method is missing.
.decl unresolvable(op: symbol, ty: symbol)
.output unresolvable
unresolvable(Op, Ty) :-
    covered(Op, T), trait_touched(Ty, T), op_method(Op, M), !impl_method(Ty, M).
